# rotunda — the room under the dome: a radiating marble floor, a giant Ionic order, four niches
#           on the diagonals with gilt urns in them, and the coffered inner dome over it all.
#
# The room the whole building is arranged around. Centred on the origin, inner radius 4.80 by the
# contract, floor at 1.80 (podium_top), and open all the way to the dome's intrados at 15.60 and
# the oculus at 17.75. Nothing in it is lit by anything except the oculus overhead and the drum's
# sixteen windows, which is the point.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT THIS PART PUTS UNDER LOAD: every indirect-light term in the renderer, in one room.
#
# There is no direct light in here at all. Every photon that lands on a surface below 15.60 has
# bounced at least once, and most of them twice. So this room is not a test of shading — it is a
# test of whether light arrives at all, and of whether what arrives is the right COLOUR.
#
#   THE COFFERS — 120 of them, in five diminishing rings of twenty-four, cut into the dome's
#   intrados by `void_rotunda`. Each is a small deep recess with three moulded steps: 0.075 (M/6),
#   0.150 (M/3) and 0.225 (M/2) deep, and the tangential step between one course and the next is
#   0.0225 — SUB-VOXEL at the contract's metre 32. The whole of a coffer's appearance is a soft
#   gradient from a bright lip to a dark pan, with two 0.0225 ledges in it. If ambient occlusion
#   is wrong, a coffer reads as a flat dark square; if the sampler loses the ledges, it reads as a
#   dimple. The rings run from 1.004 m wide at the springing to 0.309 m at the top — 10 voxels
#   across at full detail — so the same feature is asked at four scales up one surface.
#
#   THE FLOOR — porphyry, lapis, verde and two whites, laid as a radiating pattern about the
#   centre. It is directly under the oculus, it is the ONLY strongly coloured large surface in the
#   building, and the vault above it is white plaster. That is a colour-bleeding rig: if the second
#   bounce carries colour, this room's vault goes warm on the south side where the shaft lands and
#   cool where it does not, and the white marble of the order picks up red from the floor at its
#   foot. If the vault comes back neutral white, the bounce is carrying luminance and not spectrum.
#
#   THE SHAFT — the oculus is 1.80 m across (2 D) and 15.95 m above the floor. It is bound into
#   `void_rotunda` as well as the dome's own cut, so that nothing added to this building later can
#   close it by accident. The floor pattern is set out concentrically on purpose: a hard-edged
#   ellipse of sun crossing a radiating pattern is how you see whether the shaft has an edge.
#
#   THE NICHES — four semidomed hollows 1.35 m wide and 0.675 m deep, lined in green marble, with
#   a gilt urn standing on a porphyry pedestal in each. A hemisphere lit only by bounce is the
#   cleanest ambient-occlusion gradient there is, and a near-polished metal standing inside one,
#   with a coloured lining behind it, is the hardest thing in the room to get the colour of.
#
# DO NOT WIDEN THE COFFERS OR SHALLOW THEM OUT. 0.0225 is M/20 and it is meant to be under a voxel;
# 0.225 of depth in a 0.309 opening at the top ring is meant to be nearly black. If the coffers
# come back as mud the sampler is what is wrong, not the coffers. And DO NOT put a light in here.
#
# WALKABLE, AND THESE ARE THE MEASUREMENTS RATHER THAN THE INTENTION (2026-08-16).
#
# The player is 1.80 m standing, 0.60 m wide, and steps risers to 0.55. The floor to clear is
# 1.00 m of width and 2.10 m of head at any opening, 2.40 m of head in circulation, and no lip over
# 0.55 anywhere. Every figure below is `clipcheck` on the WHOLE BUILDING at metre 16 — `--gap`'s
# `clear` line, which is the longest unbroken run of air with stone at both ends, and the only one
# of the three that answers "how much head is there here" (BRIEF.md rule 7 says why `gap` does not).
# Both arms of every one of them were taken in the same session, the control from `git archive`.
#
#   what                                   probe                          before   after
#   head at the centre                     gap y@0,0                       7.812    7.812  *
#   head 3 m east                          gap y@3.0,0                    15.438   15.438
#   head 3 m north                         gap y@0,3.0                    15.438   15.438
#   head on the SE diagonal                gap y@3.0,-3.0                  9.938    9.938
#   head under a column, r 4.2             gap y@4.2,0                     9.938    9.938
#   head at the south bay's mouth          gap y@0,-4.0                   15.000   15.000
#   south doorway, clear WIDTH             gap x@-0.70,-4.94               1.750    2.750
#   south doorway, clear HEAD at x 1.2     gap y@1.2,-4.90                 3.188    3.188
#   east doorway, clear WIDTH              gap z@4.90,-0.70                2.250    2.750
#   west doorway, clear WIDTH              gap z@-4.90,-0.70               2.625    2.750
#   east doorway, clear HEAD               gap y@4.90,0                    4.062    4.062
#   north bay (BLIND), clear WIDTH         gap x@-0.70,4.94                1.500    2.750
#   floor, east-west across the building   gap x@-1.30,0                  25.750   25.750
#   floor, north-south on the axis         gap z@0,-1.30                  12.500   12.750
#   floor, chord at z = 3.0                gap x@-1.30,3.0                 7.500    7.438
#
#   * the centre column is broken by the corona lucis, which fittings.clip hangs at 9.225 — 7.425 m
#     of clear head under it, and 7.812 is the run above it that `clear` prefers. It is furniture in
#     the air, not a ceiling, and it is three times the height a person is.
#
# So: every opening is 2.75 m wide against a floor of 1.00, every head is 3.19 m or more against a
# floor of 2.40, the pavement is dead flat at 1.80 (the deepest thing cut into it is a 0.045 worn
# saucer, an eleventh of the 0.55 a player steps), and the chord at z = 3.0 lost 0.062 to the new
# wall base, which is the only number here that went the wrong way and it is a skirting board.
#
# THE THREE THAT MOVED ARE THE WHOLE POINT. 1.750 to 2.750 on the south doorway is the horns the
# deeper bay removed — see the note over `rotunda_bay_depth`. The east and west read differently
# from each other before (2.250 against 2.625) because a chord across a curved reveal quantises
# differently on each side; afterwards both are 2.750, which is the bay's own 2.70 plus a voxel.
#
# THE METRE 32 FIGURES BELOW ARE FROM BEFORE THIS AND ARE LEFT STANDING AS THE CONTROL ARM, not
# because they still describe this file. What changed: the bays went from 0.225 to 0.45 deep, the
# wall gained a 0.45 base, six course joints and a 0.030 displacement below 6.30, and the pavement
# gained ring and radial joints and three worn thresholds. Re-measured at metre 8 after the change:
# 180,409 voxels against 185,460 before (the room got bigger, so there is less stone), and the SAME
# 5 components with the same 4 floating single voxels at 11.62 — the ones this file's own note about
# metre 16 already explains. Nothing new came adrift.
#
# MEASURED, `--clip-part part_rotunda` at the contract's metre 32: 11,974,584 voxels, 365.44 m³,
# 1382.66 m² of surface, 873,787 distinct visual records, largest identical group 122, ONE
# component, and 88,205,341 field evaluations. `--clip-part rotunda_coffers` at the same metre:
# 6.88 m³ of stone taken out of the dome and 182.98 m² of surface put back in its place — twenty-
# seven times as much surface as matter — in EXACTLY 120 components. That count is the check worth
# repeating whenever anything here is touched: five rings of twenty-four, none merged into its
# neighbour and none lost. It only comes out at 120 at full detail; at metre 12 it reports 152,
# because the 0.0225 lips are a third of a voxel there and shed rings of their own.
#
# THE FIGURE TO COMPARE AGAINST IS THE EVALUATION COUNT AND NOT THE WALL CLOCK. Sampling this part
# at metre 8 took anywhere between 179 ms and 1300 ms over one session depending on how many other
# people were building at the time, for an evaluation count that never moved by a tenth of a per
# cent. I nearly filed a sampler bug against `offset` on the strength of the former; the write-up
# is in clips/facility/requests/rotunda.md and the short version is: interleave, or do not quote.
#
# AT METRE 16 THIS PART REPORTS FIVE COMPONENTS AND THAT IS THE ORDER'S URN, NOT A FLOATING PIECE
# OF MINE. The neck of `urn`'s finial is 0.048 across — one and a half voxels at metre 32, where it
# holds, and three quarters of one at metre 16, where it does not, so each of the four gilt balls
# comes away. `--clip-part rotunda_urns` gives 4 components at metre 32 and 8 at metre 16, which is
# the whole of the difference. It is a sub-voxel feature behaving like a sub-voxel feature, it is
# in a shape this file does not own, and the fix is not to fatten it here.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# THE MODULE. D = 0.90, M = 0.45, and every dimension below is one of those or a simple fraction.
# Everything vertical above 15.60 is counted in M/9 = 0.05, which is the sub-module the top of this
# building is already built in (see dome.clip). The numbers that are not module multiples, and why:
#
#   4.80          the contract's rotunda inner radius. The contract says so.
#   15.60, 2.15   drum_top, and the intrados rise: 2.60 of dome less M of crown thickness. Both
#                 come from the contract and from dome.clip's published intrados, which is the one
#                 surface this file and that one have to agree about. It is stated in that file's
#                 header as "intrados 4.80 wide, 2.15 high" and it is derived, not invented.
#   1.75, 4.85,   how far a member reaches into what it stands on. Tolerances, never seen, all of
#   5.10, 12.20   them at least the 0.05 the brief asks for.
#   3.51 .. 1.80  the radii the upper coffer rings are bounded on. Every one is a multiple of
#                 M/10 = 0.045; they are chosen so the five rings come out evenly spaced ALONG THE
#                 SURFACE, which is what the eye measures, and the arithmetic is in the table below.
#
# WHERE IT MEETS ITS NEIGHBOURS, and nothing else it needs to know:
#
#   below   the podium, whose top is 1.80. The paving is bedded 0.135 into it.
#   above   the drum, whose core is the same 4.80 inner face from 11.25 up. This wall runs to
#           12.30, so the two lap by 1.05 whatever the drum turns out to be.
#   over    the dome, whose intrados is 4.80 wide and 2.15 high on the springing at 15.60. The
#           coffers are cut out of it by `void_rotunda`, which the manifest takes away after
#           everything is assembled — which is exactly what dome.clip says it is leaving room for.
#
# WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE:
#
#   NO DOORWAY, AND THAT IS THE POINT OF THE FOUR BAYS. This part cuts no hole into anybody else's
#   ground. The four bays on the axes are recessed arches, 2.70 wide (6 M) and 4.05 to the crown,
#   and every one of them is drawn BLIND — a doorway-shaped recess in my own wall and nothing more.
#   Whoever builds a room against one of them punches through with their own `void_<part>`, which
#   the manifest takes away after this part is assembled, and changes not a line of this file.
#
#   It worked, and it was worth checking rather than assuming. The vestibule and the halls were
#   both still placeholders when this file was written; by the time it was first photographed they
#   were not, and the south bay now has a bronze door standing in it and the east and west bays
#   open into vaulted halls, none of which required anybody to read anything of mine. The north bay
#   is still blind and looks exactly as intended: a shallow arched panel, waiting.
#
#   NO DENTILS on the interior entablature. The cornice is `entab_profile` revolved, and its dentil
#   band lands at radius 4.51 — a 28.3 m circumference, which is 210 blocks at the order's 0.135
#   pitch. `around { } count=210` would take the sampler's skip away from the WHOLE CLIP (see
#   _order.clip), 210 is not a multiple of four so the quadrant-and-two-folds trick cannot express
#   it, and 210 explicit rotations is not a thing anybody should write. The band is left plain and
#   the missing language feature is written up in clips/facility/requests/rotunda.md.

include "_contract.clip"

# ================================================================================================
# THE SHELL OF THE ROOM — one section, turned
# ================================================================================================
#
# 0.90 thick (D), which is the same wall the dome springs from, from 1.75 (bedded 0.05 into the
# podium) up to 12.30, where the drum takes over. Everything else in this file is cut out of it or
# stood against it, so the whole part is one component by construction.
#
# IT STOPS AT 12.30 ON PURPOSE. The drum's sixteen windows splay through the wall between 12.7875
# and 14.8125 and they are cut inside drum.clip, not by the manifest — so any stone this file put
# in that band would plug all sixteen of them and this room would go black. Nothing here goes above
# 12.30 except the attic below, which stops at 12.75, under the lowest window sill.

let rotunda_wall_s = box 4.80 1.75 -1   5.70 12.30 1  round=0.012
let rotunda_wall_t = revolve { rotunda_wall_s } axis=y

# AND IT IS NOT A MATHEMATICALLY EXACT CYLINDER, WHICH IS THE POINT OF THIS LINE.
#
# A revolve is exact to the last decimal, and that is what a room lined in marble is NOT. Every
# real one is slabs bedded by hand on a wall that was never truly round, and what tells the eye it
# is stone rather than a surface of revolution is that the reflection of the oculus down the wall
# wanders instead of running dead straight. 0.030 is 0.96 of a voxel at the contract's metre 32 —
# just over the sampler's half-voxel floor (`usable_displacement`), so it genuinely relocates the
# surface rather than dithering it, which is the failure the manifest's own 0.012 was dropped for.
#
# `grain_stone` and not a new pattern: it is the contract's shared bed grain, so this wall is
# weathered by the same noise as everything else that uses it and there is no seam where two
# different grains meet.
#
# IT IS DISPLACED BEFORE ANYTHING IS CUT OUT OF IT AND BEFORE THE ORDER IS STOOD AGAINST IT. The
# bays, the niches and the panels are all subtracted from the union further down, so their arrises
# stay dead crisp against a wall that wanders — which is exactly right, because a moulding is
# worked and a wall face is laid. The coffers are not touched at all: they live in `void_rotunda`
# and their 0.0225 steps are the thing this file's header forbids blurring.
#
# AND IT STOPS AT 6.30, WHICH IS THE LAST COURSE JOINT BELOW THE SUNK PANELS.
#
# Measured, and this is why the line is here rather than over the whole wall: with the wander
# running the full 1.75 .. 12.30, the finished building came back with 805 components against 804
# before, and the extra one was a SINGLE VOXEL at (-3.25, 6.62, 3.50) — radius 4.78, which is the
# bottom arris of the sunk panels' margin at 6.66. The panels are cut back to a fixed radius of
# 4.935 and the wall face now arrives there anywhere between 4.77 and 4.83, so at metre 8 the step
# between the two lands inside one voxel and sheds it. Nothing was severed and the largest
# component never moved; it is one voxel of dust, and it is still a component the report has to
# print.
#
# So the wander is confined to 1.75 .. 6.30 and the wall above it is the true revolve. The step
# between the two is at most 0.030 and it falls INSIDE the course joint at 6.285 .. 6.315, which is
# a 0.030 groove — so the one place the two halves disagree is the one place there is already a
# shadow. The 4.55 m of wall the wander is on is the whole of what anybody standing on this floor
# can see closely; above 6.30 the panels, the columns and the entablature are what the eye reads,
# and none of them is a plain surface.
let rotunda_wall_wob = intersection {
    displace { rotunda_wall_t grain_stone } amount=0.030
    box -6.00 1.70 -6.00   6.00 6.30 6.00
}
let rotunda_wall_true = intersection {
    rotunda_wall_t
    box -6.00 6.28 -6.00   6.00 12.35 6.00
}
let rotunda_wall_r = union { rotunda_wall_wob rotunda_wall_true }

# THE COURSES. The marble lining is laid in beds of 0.675 (1.5 M) — walls.clip's own ashlar course
# and, six of them, the height between the string course and the column head outside. Six joints
# from the top of the base at 2.25 up to 6.30, which is the last one that clears the sunk panels
# at 6.66.
#
# CUT AND NOT PAINTED, and that is the opposite of what walls.clip decided for its own wall, on
# purpose. Out there the flatness IS the load and a painted line on a plane is the hard case. In
# here there is no direct light at all, a painted line has no shadow to be found by, and a joint
# with nothing in it would simply not exist. So this one is a groove 0.030 wide — M/15, one voxel
# at metre 32, the same number as the fillet between two flutes — and 0.12 deep in the radius,
# which is enough that it still bites through the 0.030 of wander above.
#
# ONE SECTION REVOLVED ONCE, and not six rings or a `repeat`: a `repeat` of something 0.03 tall on
# a 0.675 pitch is six shapes the sampler must settle separately, and a section costs one revolve.
let rotunda_course_sec = union {
    box 4.74 2.910 -1   4.86 2.940 1
    box 4.74 3.585 -1   4.86 3.615 1
    box 4.74 4.260 -1   4.86 4.290 1
    box 4.74 4.935 -1   4.86 4.965 1
    box 4.74 5.610 -1   4.86 5.640 1
    box 4.74 6.285 -1   4.86 6.315 1
}
let rotunda_courses = revolve { rotunda_course_sec } axis=y

let rotunda_wall = difference { rotunda_wall_r rotunda_courses }

# THE PAVING. 0.135 (0.3 M) thick, bedded into the podium, out to 5.10 so that the floor of a bay
# recess is paved too. Its top is the contract's podium_top, which is the floor of the building.
let rotunda_floor = cylinder 0 1.7325 0  r=5.10 h=0.135 axis=y

# ================================================================================================
# AND IT IS LAID IN SEPARATE STONES, WITH JOINTS — which is the difference between a pavement and
# a picture of one
# ================================================================================================
#
# Everything below this line was a PAINTED pattern and nothing else: six concentric colours and
# forty-eight coloured bands, on one unbroken disc of stone 10.20 m across. In a raking shaft of
# sun off the oculus that reads as a printed floorcloth, because the one thing a real pavement does
# under a low light and a painted one cannot is throw a hairline of shadow along every joint.
#
# So the joints are cut. 0.030 wide (M/15 — one voxel at the contract's metre 32, the width this
# building already uses for a mortar line) and 0.030 deep, which leaves 0.105 of the 0.135 slab
# under them, so nothing here can sever the paving from itself.
#
# THEY GO IN `void_rotunda` AND NOT IN A `difference` UP HERE, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE TRICK.
#
# The first version of this cut them out of `rotunda_floor` and they DID NOT EXIST. This paving is
# 0.135 of stone bedded into a podium that is solid to 1.80 underneath it — measured, `part_podium`
# fills 100.00% of the band y 1.72 .. 1.80 across the whole room, and `part_crypt` another 64.75% —
# so a groove taken out of my slab is immediately backfilled by somebody else's, and the floor comes
# back exactly as flat as it was with nothing to show for the arithmetic. A hollow in the FLOOR OF A
# ROOM is room air by definition, and room air is what a `void_` is for. The same is true of every
# other pavement in this building and it is why this note is longer than the shapes it explains.
#
# Bounded to r <= 5.10, which is my own paving's edge. Between 4.95 and 5.10 on the east and west
# axes that is also the lip of halls.clip's floor slab, and a joint 0.030 deep runs across it — as
# a joint at a threshold does in any building. Nothing structural is in reach: the deepest thing
# here is 0.045 and the crypt's soffit is 0.1125 below the shallowest of it.
#
# THE RINGS are the six radii the colour already changes at, so a joint falls exactly where one
# stone stops and the next begins rather than somewhere near it: 0.90, 1.035, 1.35, 3.60, 3.735 and
# 4.50, and a seventh at 4.86 dividing the border from the threshold stones.
#
# THE RADIALS are the same twenty-four lines the coffers overhead and the porphyry spokes below are
# set out on, drawn the same way — one quadrant of seven rotations, folded twice, negative angles —
# so the floor is jointed on the figure it is painted on. They run from 0.90 out to 5.10 and stop
# at the middle roundel, which is one stone.
let rotunda_fj_ring_one = union {
    difference { cylinder 0 1.80 0 r=0.915 h=0.06 axis=y  cylinder 0 1.80 0 r=0.885 h=0.08 axis=y }
    difference { cylinder 0 1.80 0 r=1.050 h=0.06 axis=y  cylinder 0 1.80 0 r=1.020 h=0.08 axis=y }
    difference { cylinder 0 1.80 0 r=1.365 h=0.06 axis=y  cylinder 0 1.80 0 r=1.335 h=0.08 axis=y }
    difference { cylinder 0 1.80 0 r=3.615 h=0.06 axis=y  cylinder 0 1.80 0 r=3.585 h=0.08 axis=y }
    difference { cylinder 0 1.80 0 r=3.750 h=0.06 axis=y  cylinder 0 1.80 0 r=3.720 h=0.08 axis=y }
    difference { cylinder 0 1.80 0 r=4.515 h=0.06 axis=y  cylinder 0 1.80 0 r=4.485 h=0.08 axis=y }
    difference { cylinder 0 1.80 0 r=4.875 h=0.06 axis=y  cylinder 0 1.80 0 r=4.845 h=0.08 axis=y }
}

let rotunda_fj_rad   = box 0.88 1.770 -0.015   5.12 1.810 0.015
let rotunda_fj_rad_b = rotate { rotunda_fj_rad } y=-0.0416667
let rotunda_fj_rad_c = rotate { rotunda_fj_rad } y=-0.0833333
let rotunda_fj_rad_d = rotate { rotunda_fj_rad } y=-0.125
let rotunda_fj_rad_e = rotate { rotunda_fj_rad } y=-0.1666667
let rotunda_fj_rad_f = rotate { rotunda_fj_rad } y=-0.2083333
let rotunda_fj_rad_g = rotate { rotunda_fj_rad } y=-0.25
let rotunda_fj_radials = intersection {
    mirror { mirror { union { rotunda_fj_rad rotunda_fj_rad_b rotunda_fj_rad_c rotunda_fj_rad_d
                              rotunda_fj_rad_e rotunda_fj_rad_f rotunda_fj_rad_g } } axis=x } axis=z
    box -5.15 1.76 -5.15   5.15 1.82 5.15
}

# ================================================================================================
# THE THRESHOLDS — the three stones a foot actually lands on, dished by the feet that landed there
# ================================================================================================
#
# A threshold in a building that has been used is the one piece of floor you can date by looking at
# it: the arris is gone, the polish is gone, and there is a saucer worn into the middle of it two
# or three centimetres deep. Nothing else in this building carries that, and it is the cheapest
# true thing a scanned interior has that a modelled one does not.
#
# 0.045 (M/10) at the deepest, over a dish 2.10 across and 0.60 through — so it is a saucer and not
# a step: the player's own limit is a 0.55 riser and this is an eighth of one, and it slopes.
#
# THREE AND NOT FOUR, and that is the whole point of cutting them by hand instead of folding them.
# The south bay is the great door, the east and west bays are the halls, and the north bay is
# BLIND — this file's header says so twice. Wearing a hollow into the floor of a recess nobody can
# walk out of would say the opposite of what wear is for. So: one at +x, mirrored to -x, and one
# rotated a POSITIVE quarter turn, which carries +x to -z and is the one place in this file a
# positive angle is right, because nothing folds it afterwards to throw it away.
let rotunda_thr_one   = ellipsoid 4.80 2.205 0  rx=0.30 ry=0.45 rz=1.05
let rotunda_thr_south = rotate { rotunda_thr_one } y=0.25
let rotunda_thresholds = intersection {
    union { mirror { rotunda_thr_one } axis=x   rotunda_thr_south }
    box -5.15 1.74 -5.15   5.15 1.82 5.15
}

# Everything taken out of the pavement, in one binding, clipped to my own paving's own radius.
let rotunda_floor_cuts = intersection {
    union { rotunda_fj_ring_one rotunda_fj_radials rotunda_thresholds }
    cylinder 0 1.79 0  r=5.10 h=0.10 axis=y
}

# THE ATTIC, and it is here to close a hole that is not otherwise closed by anybody.
#
# roof.clip leaves a SQUARE well 12.00 x 12.00 for the drum to stand in and says so in capitals: a
# round drum in a square hole leaves the four corners open — 31 m² of sky straight down into the
# block — and that a square attic pedestal under the drum is the classical answer and the one the
# roof is drawn for. This is that pedestal. It is 12.10 square (±6.05, lapping the roof's kerb by
# 0.05), it runs from 12.20 — clear of the drum's copper apron, which tops out at 12.15 — to 12.75,
# which is under the lowest drum window sill at 12.7875, and it is bored to the room's own 4.80.
#
# It is invisible: the parapet stands at 13.00 and nothing here reaches it.
let rotunda_attic = difference {
    box -6.05 12.20 -6.05   6.05 12.75 6.05  round=0.02
    cylinder 0 12.475 0  r=4.80 h=1.00 axis=y
}

# ================================================================================================
# THE BASE OF THE WALL — 0.45 m (M) of moulding at the foot, round the whole room
# ================================================================================================
#
# There was nothing here. The marble wall met the marble pavement on a butt joint with no member
# between them, which is the one thing no room of this order has ever been built with: a wall base
# is where the eye is told how thick the wall is, and without one the room reads as a cylinder of
# paper standing on a disc.
#
# Three members, 0.45 (M) tall in all, reckoned up from the floor at 1.80 and each a module
# fraction, drawn ONCE as a section in (radius, height) and revolved — which is the only
# construction that makes a torus a torus round a room 9.60 m across:
#
#   1.800 - 2.025   plinth     M/2      the die, standing 0.135 (0.3 M) proud of the wall face
#   2.025 - 2.115   torus      M/5      a roll of 0.045 (M/10) on the plinth's face, out to 0.18
#   2.115 - 2.250   congé      0.3 M    a cavetto dying back into the wall face at 4.80
#
# The core runs out to 4.88, which is 0.08 INSIDE the wall face, so the base is bedded into the
# wall it stands against rather than coincident with it — and it is 0.05 into the paving at the
# bottom for the same reason. Both are the brief's overlap and neither is ever seen.
#
# IT IS CUT BY THE BAYS AND NOT BY THE NICHES, and both are right. A bay is a doorway and its
# reveal goes to the floor, so the base returns into the jamb and stops — which happens for free,
# because the bay cutter starts at radius 4.10, well in front of anything here. A niche sills at
# 2.70, which is D above the floor and 0.45 above the top of this, so the base runs unbroken behind
# all four of them, which is what a niche at that height is for.
let rotunda_base_core  = box     4.665 1.75 -1   4.88 2.115 1
let rotunda_base_roll  = bead    4.665 2.025   4.62  2.115
let rotunda_base_conge = cavetto 4.80  2.115   4.665 2.25
let rotunda_base_sec   = union { rotunda_base_core rotunda_base_roll rotunda_base_conge }
let rotunda_base       = revolve { rotunda_base_sec } axis=y

# ================================================================================================
# THE FOUR BAYS ON THE AXES — recessed arches, 2.70 wide, 0.45 deep
# ================================================================================================
#
# Springing 4.50, crown 5.85 on a 1.35 (3 M) radius, sill on the floor: 2.70 wide (6 M), 2.70 clear
# to the springing and 4.05 to the crown. The brief's door head is 2.40 and this clears it across
# the whole width, so a doorway fits one without anything here moving.
#
# The back of the recess is a CYLINDER concentric with the wall, not a plane, so
# the recess is 0.45 deep at the middle and 0.45 deep at the jambs; a flat back on a curved wall
# is 0.42 deep in the corners and reads as a wedge.
#
# 0.45 (M) DEEP AND NOT 0.225, AND THAT IS A WALKABILITY FIX BEFORE IT IS AN ELEVATION ONE.
#
# A neighbour who opens one of these bays punches through with a void of their own, and a void is a
# BOX or a prism: it stops on a flat plane. My wall is a CYLINDER. Where the two meet, whatever
# lies between the neighbour's last plane and my recess is stone nobody meant to leave, and because
# a chord is shortest in the middle and longest at the ends it is left as a pair of curved horns
# growing in from the jambs.
#
# Measured on the finished building at waist height, x-run across the south doorway, with the
# recess at 0.225: the vestibule's void stops at z = -4.95 and the opening between the horns came
# out 1.750 m clear against a bay that is 2.70 m wide — a third of the doorway gone, and it read as
# a doorway that had been narrowed on purpose. At 0.45 the recess reaches past the neighbour's
# plane everywhere across the full 2.70, the horns cannot exist, and the same measurement is
# 2.750 m. Nothing else changed and no other fragment was touched.
#
# It leaves 0.45 of wall behind the recess, which is the same 1 M the screen's entablature is deep,
# and it cannot reach anybody: `rotunda_bays` is subtracted INSIDE this file, from my own stone.
let rotunda_bay_body  = box 4.10 1.80 -1.35   5.30 4.50 1.35
let rotunda_bay_head  = cylinder 4.70 4.50 0  r=1.35 h=1.20 axis=x
let rotunda_bay_depth = cylinder 0 3.80 0  r=5.25 h=4.40 axis=y
let rotunda_bay_one   = intersection {
    union { rotunda_bay_body rotunda_bay_head }
    rotunda_bay_depth
}

# ================================================================================================
# THE FOUR NICHES ON THE DIAGONALS
# ================================================================================================
#
# Semicircular in plan on a 0.675 (1.5 M) radius struck on the wall face, so the opening is 1.35
# wide (3 M) and the hollow is 0.675 deep, leaving 0.225 of wall behind it. Sill 2.70 (D above the
# floor), springing 5.40, and a hemispherical head on the same radius crowning at 6.075.
#
# The cutter is a whole cylinder and a whole sphere and it is NOT clipped to the wall face. It does
# not need to be: the half of it that stands in the room has no stone in it to remove, and clipping
# it against a plane at x = 4.80 would leave a 0.05 lip of wall standing across the jambs, because
# the wall face is a cylinder of radius 4.80 and a plane is not.
let rotunda_niche_cut_one = union {
    cylinder 4.80 4.05 0  r=0.675 h=2.70 axis=y
    sphere   4.80 5.40 0  r=0.675
}

# THE ARCHITRAVE ROUND IT. The shell between a cutter 0.270 (0.6 M) larger and the cutter itself,
# taken where it crosses a ring at the wall face: that is a band 0.27 wide standing 0.135 proud,
# following the jambs up and round the arch and returning across the sill as its base moulding,
# and it needs no arithmetic of its own to do it. A flat plate would have stood 0.135 proud at the
# middle and 0.029 at the jambs, which is where the first pass of this went; and at 0.18 wide it
# was two voxels of white marble against a white marble wall and might as well not have been cut.
let rotunda_niche_big = union {
    cylinder 4.80 3.915 0  r=0.945 h=2.97 axis=y
    sphere   4.80 5.40  0  r=0.945
}
let rotunda_niche_face = revolve { box 4.665 2.30 -1   4.95 6.50 1  round=0.010 } axis=y
let rotunda_niche_dress_one = intersection {
    difference { rotunda_niche_big rotunda_niche_cut_one }
    rotunda_niche_face
}

# THE PEDESTAL, 1.35 (3 M) tall: plinth M/2, die 2 M, cap M/2. It stands on the niche floor and is
# bedded 0.05 into it, and it is unioned back on AFTER the niche is cut, which is the only order
# that works — the cutter would eat it otherwise.
let rotunda_ped_plinth = box 4.635 2.65  -0.315   5.265 2.925 0.315  round=0.012
let rotunda_ped_die    = box 4.680 2.925 -0.270   5.220 3.825 0.270  round=0.012
let rotunda_ped_cap    = box 4.635 3.825 -0.315   5.265 4.050 0.315  round=0.012
let rotunda_ped_one    = union { rotunda_ped_plinth rotunda_ped_die rotunda_ped_cap }

# THE URN. The order's own, 1.35 tall, standing 4.05 to 5.40 — so its lip is exactly on the
# springing of the niche head and the hemisphere is its canopy.
let rotunda_urn_one = translate { urn } 4.95 4.05 0

# ================================================================================================
# THE SUNK PANELS over the bays and the niches
# ================================================================================================
#
# 1.80 (4 M) tall, between 6.75 (15 M) and 8.55 (19 M) — above every arch head and below the
# architrave, filling the one stretch of this room that has nothing else in it. Cut back to a
# cylinder for the same reason the bays are.
#
# TWO STEPS AND NOT ONE. A panel sunk a single 0.09 into a white wall in a room with no direct
# light in it is a panel nobody can see; what makes a sunk panel read is the shadow in the rebate
# round it, so there is a 0.09 (0.2 M) margin taken back 0.135 and the field inside it taken back
# 0.225. That is the same 0.135/0.225 pair the coffers use, for the same reason.
let rotunda_panel_dep_a = cylinder 0 7.65 0  r=4.935 h=2.40 axis=y
let rotunda_panel_dep_b = cylinder 0 7.65 0  r=5.025 h=2.40 axis=y
let rotunda_panel_b_one = union {
    intersection { box 4.60 6.66 -1.44   5.10 8.64 1.44  round=0.012   rotunda_panel_dep_a }
    intersection { box 4.60 6.75 -1.35   5.10 8.55 1.35  round=0.012   rotunda_panel_dep_b }
}
let rotunda_panel_n_one = union {
    intersection { box 4.60 6.66 -0.765  5.10 8.64 0.765 round=0.012   rotunda_panel_dep_a }
    intersection { box 4.60 6.75 -0.675  5.10 8.55 0.675 round=0.012   rotunda_panel_dep_b }
}

# ================================================================================================
# SETTING THEM OUT — eight bays, and why every angle is negative
# ================================================================================================
#
# Four bays on the axes and four niches on the diagonals, with a column of the order on each of the
# eight boundaries between them, at ±22.5° and ±67.5°.
#
# Laid out as explicit `rotate` nodes over one quadrant and mirrored in x and then in z, and NEVER
# as `around { } count=8`. `metric_slack(Op::PolarRepeat)` is infinite, the sampler takes the
# minimum slack over the whole expression, and infinite slack anywhere means not one voxel of the
# clip's bounds can be skipped ANYWHERE — _order.clip measured that substitution on a single column
# at a factor of 950 in field evaluations. Rotations are isometries and are exact.
#
# THE ANGLES ARE NEGATIVE AND THAT IS NOT A TYPO. `mirror` folds a coordinate to its POSITIVE side,
# so whatever lies on the negative side of a fold is not copied, it is thrown away; and
# `rotate { } y=+turns` carries +x toward -z. Write the quadrant with positive angles, as it reads
# naturally, and every member lands on -z and is silently deleted by the second mirror. Measured
# twice already, in drum.clip and in dome.clip, and written here a third time because it costs an
# afternoon every time somebody meets it.
#
# AND EVERY FAN IS WRAPPED IN AN INTERSECTION WITH A BOX. `build_bounds` gives `Op::Rotate` no
# bounding box at all, so a union of rotated things reports infinite bounds and nothing upstream —
# including the sampler's own settling — can measure it. A box it already fits inside hands the
# bounds back and changes not one voxel. The boxes below are bounds, not cutters; nothing touches
# their faces.

# THE BAY BOX IS +-5.40 AND IT USED TO BE +-5.15, WHICH WAS EXACTLY THE OLD RECESS DEPTH PLUS A
# TOLERANCE. These boxes are bounds and not cutters — the note above says nothing touches their
# faces — so deepening the recess to 5.25 without moving this one would have had the box quietly
# doing the cutting instead, and the horns the deeper recess exists to remove would have come
# straight back at a different radius.
let rotunda_box_bay   = box -5.40 1.70  -5.40   5.40 5.95  5.40
let rotunda_box_niche = box -5.90 2.55  -5.90   5.90 6.20  5.90
let rotunda_box_dress = box -5.00 2.25  -5.00   5.00 6.55  5.00
let rotunda_box_ped   = box -5.40 2.60  -5.40   5.40 5.50  5.40
let rotunda_box_col   = box -5.80 1.70  -5.80   5.80 10.00 5.80
let rotunda_box_panel = box -5.10 6.60  -5.10   5.10 8.70  5.10

# The four bays: the one on +x, and the same turned a quarter so it lands on +z.
let rotunda_bay_two = rotate { rotunda_bay_one } y=-0.25
let rotunda_bays = intersection {
    mirror { mirror { union { rotunda_bay_one rotunda_bay_two } } axis=x } axis=z
    rotunda_box_bay
}

# The four niches, on the diagonals: one member at 45°, folded twice.
let rotunda_niche_r = rotate { rotunda_niche_cut_one } y=-0.125
let rotunda_niches = intersection {
    mirror { mirror { rotunda_niche_r } axis=x } axis=z
    rotunda_box_niche
}

let rotunda_dress_r = rotate { rotunda_niche_dress_one } y=-0.125
let rotunda_dressings = intersection {
    mirror { mirror { rotunda_dress_r } axis=x } axis=z
    rotunda_box_dress
}

let rotunda_ped_r = rotate { rotunda_ped_one } y=-0.125
let rotunda_pedestals = intersection {
    mirror { mirror { rotunda_ped_r } axis=x } axis=z
    rotunda_box_ped
}

let rotunda_urn_r = rotate { rotunda_urn_one } y=-0.125
let rotunda_urns = intersection {
    mirror { mirror { rotunda_urn_r } axis=x } axis=z
    rotunda_box_ped
}

# The panels: the wide ones over the four bays, the narrow ones over the four niches.
let rotunda_panel_b_two = rotate { rotunda_panel_b_one } y=-0.25
let rotunda_panel_n_r   = rotate { rotunda_panel_n_one } y=-0.125
let rotunda_panels = intersection {
    mirror { mirror { union { rotunda_panel_b_one rotunda_panel_b_two rotunda_panel_n_r } } axis=x } axis=z
    rotunda_box_panel
}

# ================================================================================================
# THE GIANT ORDER — eight engaged Ionic columns, 1.80 to 9.90
# ================================================================================================
#
# The order's own `ionic_column`, 8.10 tall (9 D), standing on the floor at the contract's
# column_foot and topping out at its column_head — the same two levels as the six columns of the
# portico outside, which is what makes the inside of this building the same building as the
# outside. The axes stand at radius 4.95, so a third of each shaft is engaged in the wall and the
# other two thirds stand clear of it: the column is held along its whole height and cannot float,
# and the flutes on the free side still catch the light coming down the oculus edge-on.
let rotunda_col_one = translate { ionic_column } 4.95 1.80 0
let rotunda_col_a   = rotate { rotunda_col_one } y=-0.0625
let rotunda_col_b   = rotate { rotunda_col_one } y=-0.1875
let rotunda_columns = intersection {
    mirror { mirror { union { rotunda_col_a rotunda_col_b } } axis=x } axis=z
    rotunda_box_col
}

# ================================================================================================
# THE ENTABLATURE — 9.90 to 11.90, the contract's 2.00, turned
# ================================================================================================
#
# `entab_profile` is drawn as a section in (projection, height) with the wall at x = 0 and the
# projection running OUT along +x, which is what an outside wall wants. An inside wall wants it the
# other way about, so it is turned half a turn about y — which maps x to -x and leaves the section
# in its own plane — and set down at radius 4.85, 0.05 inside the wall face so there is no hairline
# for the manifest's grain to open. It then projects INWARD to 4.13.
#
# The intersection is not decoration: `rotate` carries no bounding box, and a `revolve` of something
# with infinite bounds is a shape the sampler cannot settle anywhere in the clip.
let rotunda_entab_sec = intersection {
    translate { rotate { entab_profile } y=0.5 } 4.85 9.90 0
    box 4.05 9.85 -1.10   4.90 11.95 1.10
}
let rotunda_entab = revolve { rotunda_entab_sec } axis=y

# ================================================================================================
# THE INNER DOME — five diminishing rings of twenty-four coffers
# ================================================================================================
#
# The intrados is dome.clip's: an ellipsoid 4.80 wide and 2.15 high standing on the springing at
# 15.60, both of which come from the contract by way of that file's published header. The coffers
# are cut out of the 0.90 m of shell behind it (0.45 at the crown) by `void_rotunda`, which the
# manifest takes away after the whole building is assembled — so they cut the dome without this
# file and that one sharing a single binding.
#
# A COFFER IS NOT MODELLED AND PLACED. It is what is left when three nested surfaces are banded and
# the ribs are put back, which is the same construction the flutes of a column are cut by and it is
# the only one that follows a doubly curved surface without an arithmetic per coffer:
#
#   THE STEPS      three ellipsoids concentric with the intrados, each 0.075 (M/6) larger in both
#                  semi-axes, so the recess is 0.075, 0.150 and 0.225 deep and the depth is the
#                  same at the springing as at the crown.
#   THE RINGS      the two lower ones bounded in HEIGHT, the three upper ones bounded in RADIUS,
#                  and that is not inconsistency, it is conditioning: near the springing the
#                  surface is nearly vertical and a horizontal plane cuts it crisply while a
#                  cylinder cuts it tangentially, and near the crown it is exactly the other way
#                  round. Each step's band is inset 0.045 (M/10) from the one outside it.
#   THE RIBS       twenty-four slabs through the axis, 0.315 (0.7 M) thick at the pan and shrunk by
#                  `offset` to 0.270 and 0.225 for the two courses above it. A slab through the
#                  axis is the same thickness at every radius, so the ribs stay constant and the
#                  COFFERS diminish on their own as the rings close in. One fan of seven rotations
#                  does all three courses; that is what `offset` is for.
#
# The set-out, measured along the surface from the springing — which is what the eye measures, not
# height and not radius:
#
#   ring   bound            arc height   opening        rib below it
#   1      y 15.85 .. 16.45     0.707     1.004 x 0.707     0.253
#   2      y 16.60 .. 16.95     0.622     0.840 x 0.622     0.219
#   3      r  3.51 ..  3.06     0.541     0.643 x 0.541     0.265
#   4      r  2.79 ..  2.34     0.468     0.463 x 0.468     0.222
#   5      r  2.16 ..  1.80     0.367     0.309 x 0.367     0.185
#
# and the top of ring 5 stands at radius 1.80, which is 0.63 clear of the 1.17 inner edge of the
# dome's bronze oculus ring — so no coffer is ever cut in half by the hole.
#
# RINGS 4 AND 5 HAVE TWO COURSES AND NOT THREE. At 0.309 of opening a third course would leave a
# pan 0.219 wide and 0.225 deep, which is a drilled hole and not a coffer. Diminishing the depth
# with the width is also what the shell wants, since it thins to M at the crown.

let rotunda_int   = ellipsoid 0 15.60 0  rx=4.800 ry=2.150 rz=4.800
let rotunda_int_a = ellipsoid 0 15.60 0  rx=4.875 ry=2.225 rz=4.875
let rotunda_int_b = ellipsoid 0 15.60 0  rx=4.950 ry=2.300 rz=4.950
let rotunda_int_c = ellipsoid 0 15.60 0  rx=5.025 ry=2.375 rz=5.025

let rotunda_shell_a = difference { rotunda_int_a rotunda_int }
let rotunda_shell_b = difference { rotunda_int_b rotunda_int }
let rotunda_shell_c = difference { rotunda_int_c rotunda_int }

# The twenty-four ribs. Negative angles, quadrant, folded twice — see the note above. The fan is
# boxed because `rotate` reports no bounds, and then shrunk twice rather than rebuilt twice.
let rotunda_cof_fin = box 0.30 15.55 -0.1575   5.20 18.10 0.1575  round=0.010
let rotunda_cof_b   = rotate { rotunda_cof_fin } y=-0.0416667
let rotunda_cof_c   = rotate { rotunda_cof_fin } y=-0.0833333
let rotunda_cof_d   = rotate { rotunda_cof_fin } y=-0.125
let rotunda_cof_e   = rotate { rotunda_cof_fin } y=-0.1666667
let rotunda_cof_f   = rotate { rotunda_cof_fin } y=-0.2083333
let rotunda_cof_g   = rotate { rotunda_cof_fin } y=-0.25
let rotunda_cof_quad = union {
    rotunda_cof_fin rotunda_cof_b rotunda_cof_c rotunda_cof_d
    rotunda_cof_e rotunda_cof_f rotunda_cof_g
}
let rotunda_rib_c = intersection {
    mirror { mirror { rotunda_cof_quad } axis=x } axis=z
    box -5.25 15.50 -5.25   5.25 18.15 5.25
}
let rotunda_rib_b = offset { rotunda_rib_c } by=0.0225
let rotunda_rib_a = offset { rotunda_rib_c } by=0.045

# The bands. Two rings bounded in height, three bounded in radius, each course inset 0.045.
let rotunda_bnd_1a = box -5.10 15.850 -5.10   5.10 16.450 5.10
let rotunda_bnd_1b = box -5.10 15.895 -5.10   5.10 16.405 5.10
let rotunda_bnd_1c = box -5.10 15.940 -5.10   5.10 16.360 5.10

let rotunda_bnd_2a = box -5.10 16.600 -5.10   5.10 16.950 5.10
let rotunda_bnd_2b = box -5.10 16.645 -5.10   5.10 16.905 5.10
let rotunda_bnd_2c = box -5.10 16.690 -5.10   5.10 16.860 5.10

let rotunda_bnd_3a = difference { cylinder 0 16.9 0 r=3.510 h=3.0 axis=y   cylinder 0 16.9 0 r=3.060 h=3.2 axis=y }
let rotunda_bnd_3b = difference { cylinder 0 16.9 0 r=3.465 h=3.0 axis=y   cylinder 0 16.9 0 r=3.105 h=3.2 axis=y }
let rotunda_bnd_3c = difference { cylinder 0 16.9 0 r=3.420 h=3.0 axis=y   cylinder 0 16.9 0 r=3.150 h=3.2 axis=y }

let rotunda_bnd_4a = difference { cylinder 0 16.9 0 r=2.790 h=3.0 axis=y   cylinder 0 16.9 0 r=2.340 h=3.2 axis=y }
let rotunda_bnd_4b = difference { cylinder 0 16.9 0 r=2.745 h=3.0 axis=y   cylinder 0 16.9 0 r=2.385 h=3.2 axis=y }

let rotunda_bnd_5a = difference { cylinder 0 16.9 0 r=2.160 h=3.0 axis=y   cylinder 0 16.9 0 r=1.800 h=3.2 axis=y }
let rotunda_bnd_5b = difference { cylinder 0 16.9 0 r=2.115 h=3.0 axis=y   cylinder 0 16.9 0 r=1.845 h=3.2 axis=y }

# One course of one ring: the shell of that depth, cut to the band, with the ribs taken out.
let rotunda_cof_1a = difference { intersection { rotunda_shell_a rotunda_bnd_1a } rotunda_rib_a }
let rotunda_cof_1b = difference { intersection { rotunda_shell_b rotunda_bnd_1b } rotunda_rib_b }
let rotunda_cof_1c = difference { intersection { rotunda_shell_c rotunda_bnd_1c } rotunda_rib_c }

let rotunda_cof_2a = difference { intersection { rotunda_shell_a rotunda_bnd_2a } rotunda_rib_a }
let rotunda_cof_2b = difference { intersection { rotunda_shell_b rotunda_bnd_2b } rotunda_rib_b }
let rotunda_cof_2c = difference { intersection { rotunda_shell_c rotunda_bnd_2c } rotunda_rib_c }

let rotunda_cof_3a = difference { intersection { rotunda_shell_a rotunda_bnd_3a } rotunda_rib_a }
let rotunda_cof_3b = difference { intersection { rotunda_shell_b rotunda_bnd_3b } rotunda_rib_b }
let rotunda_cof_3c = difference { intersection { rotunda_shell_c rotunda_bnd_3c } rotunda_rib_c }

let rotunda_cof_4a = difference { intersection { rotunda_shell_a rotunda_bnd_4a } rotunda_rib_a }
let rotunda_cof_4b = difference { intersection { rotunda_shell_b rotunda_bnd_4b } rotunda_rib_b }

let rotunda_cof_5a = difference { intersection { rotunda_shell_a rotunda_bnd_5a } rotunda_rib_a }
let rotunda_cof_5b = difference { intersection { rotunda_shell_b rotunda_bnd_5b } rotunda_rib_b }

let rotunda_coffers = intersection {
    union {
        rotunda_cof_1a rotunda_cof_1b rotunda_cof_1c
        rotunda_cof_2a rotunda_cof_2b rotunda_cof_2c
        rotunda_cof_3a rotunda_cof_3b rotunda_cof_3c
        rotunda_cof_4a rotunda_cof_4b
        rotunda_cof_5a rotunda_cof_5b
    }
    box -5.10 15.60 -5.10   5.10 18.05 5.10
}

# ================================================================================================
# THE OCULUS — 1.80 m (2 D) across, and it stays open
# ================================================================================================
#
# dome.clip cuts this hole itself. It is bound here as well, and taken away by the manifest after
# everything in the building is assembled, so that nothing added to this scene later can close the
# one aperture the whole interior is lit through. The brief for this part asks for exactly that.
#
# IT IS CLIPPED AT 18.65 AND THAT IS LOAD-BEARING. The splay is a cone that comes to a point at
# y = 20.00, which is above the lantern; unclipped it would swallow the lantern's neck and its
# gilt ball, which are 0.045 and 0.09 across and sit on the axis at 19.40 and 19.51. Below 18.65
# there is nothing on the axis but hole.
let rotunda_oc_bore  = cylinder 0 17.875 0  r=0.90 h=1.55 axis=y
let rotunda_oc_splay = cone     0 17.300 0  r=1.35 h=2.70 axis=y
let rotunda_oculus   = intersection {
    union { rotunda_oc_bore rotunda_oc_splay }
    box -1.40 17.05 -1.40   1.40 18.65 1.40
}

# ================================================================================================
# ASSEMBLY
# ================================================================================================
#
# The stone first, then everything cut out of it, and only then the three things that stand INSIDE
# what was cut and must not be eaten by it: the pedestals, the urns and the architraves round the
# niches. Everything hangs off the wall, which runs unbroken from 1.75 to 12.30, so this is one
# component and there is nothing in it that can come adrift.
let rotunda_mass = union { rotunda_wall rotunda_floor rotunda_base rotunda_attic }
let rotunda_cut  = difference {
    union { rotunda_mass rotunda_entab rotunda_columns }
    rotunda_bays rotunda_niches rotunda_panels
}

let part_rotunda = union {
    rotunda_cut
    rotunda_dressings
    rotunda_pedestals
    rotunda_urns
}

# What this part removes from the rest of the building: the coffers, which are cut out of the
# dome's shell; the oculus, which must stay open; and the joints and worn thresholds in the
# pavement, which are room air over a podium that is solid underneath — see the long note over the
# joints for why they cannot be a `difference` in my own slab. Nothing else — the bays, the niches
# and the panels are cut out of my own stone above, inside this file, because a niche is not a room
# and there is nothing else in this building for one to hollow.
#
# NONE OF THE THREE CAN REACH A FITTING. The manifest unions `part_fittings` back on AFTER every
# room void for the reason D608 is written up for, and in any case the deepest thing in here at
# floor level is 0.045 and the lowest fitting in this room hangs at 9.225.
let void_rotunda = union { rotunda_coffers rotunda_oculus rotunda_floor_cuts }

# ================================================================================================
# PAINT
# ================================================================================================
#
# Every rule keys on a shape bound in this file and every one tests below=0.02 and not below=0,
# because the manifest displaces the whole building by 12 mm of grain at the end and a voxel that
# exists only because the grain pushed the surface out lies OUTSIDE the shape that made it. At
# below=0 those voxels match nothing, fall back to the base coat, and leave a pale limestone rind
# over a marble room.
#
# Order matters — a later coat covers an earlier one — and the order here is: the room, then the
# floor from the rim inwards, then the hollows, then what stands in them.

paint marble where=rotunda_cut below=0.02
paint marble where=rotunda_entab below=0.02
paint marble where=rotunda_columns below=0.02
paint limestone where=rotunda_attic below=0.02

# THE FRIEZE, IN PORPHYRY, AND IT IS THERE FOR THE VAULT AND NOT FOR ITSELF.
#
# `entab_profile`'s frieze is the plain band between 0.65 and 1.25 of its own height, which lands
# at 10.55 to 11.15 with its face at 4.71. Seventeen square metres of dark red, running right round
# the room, four and a half metres below the springing and with nothing between it and the white
# plaster of the dome. The floor is the larger coloured surface and it is the one the shaft lands
# on, but it is fifteen metres from the vault and half of it is white; this band is the one that
# will show first if the second bounce carries colour, and it is the one to look at.
#
# The zone is a ring of my own at the frieze's exact height and projection. Nothing else in this
# building is at radius 4.68 to 4.90 between 10.55 and 11.15 — the columns stop at 9.90 — so it
# cannot reach anybody's work but mine.
let rotunda_frieze_zone = revolve { box 4.68 10.55 -1   4.90 11.15 1 } axis=y
paint porphyry where=rotunda_frieze_zone below=0.02

# THE FLOOR. This is the colour in the building. It is directly under the oculus, it is the only
# large strongly coloured surface anywhere in the scene, and the vault over it is white — so what
# it is really for is to be looked for on the vault rather than on itself.
#
# Every zone is a slice of the paving and nothing else, so no rule can reach the wall standing on
# it: they are all 0.135 tall and they all stop at 1.80.
#
# THE DISC IN THE MIDDLE IS WHITE AND THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT OF THE PATTERN. The oculus is 1.80
# across and it is on this axis; when the sun is high the shaft lands inside r = 0.90, so the one
# place in the building that ever sees direct light is the one place laid in plain white marble.
# Everything coloured is arranged around it, close enough to be lit by the splash and to throw its
# colour back up at the vault. A dark porphyry roundel here — which is what the first pass had —
# swallows the shaft, and then there is no bounce to carry any colour anywhere.
let rotunda_fl_disc    = cylinder 0 1.7325 0  r=0.900 h=0.14 axis=y
let rotunda_fl_ring_in = difference { cylinder 0 1.7325 0 r=1.035 h=0.14 axis=y
                                      cylinder 0 1.7325 0 r=0.900 h=0.16 axis=y }
let rotunda_fl_star    = difference { cylinder 0 1.7325 0 r=1.350 h=0.14 axis=y
                                      cylinder 0 1.7325 0 r=1.035 h=0.16 axis=y }
let rotunda_fl_field   = difference { cylinder 0 1.7325 0 r=3.600 h=0.14 axis=y
                                      cylinder 0 1.7325 0 r=1.350 h=0.16 axis=y }
let rotunda_fl_ring_out= difference { cylinder 0 1.7325 0 r=3.735 h=0.14 axis=y
                                      cylinder 0 1.7325 0 r=3.600 h=0.16 axis=y }
let rotunda_fl_outer   = difference { cylinder 0 1.7325 0 r=4.500 h=0.14 axis=y
                                      cylinder 0 1.7325 0 r=3.735 h=0.16 axis=y }
let rotunda_fl_border  = difference { cylinder 0 1.7325 0 r=5.100 h=0.14 axis=y
                                      cylinder 0 1.7325 0 r=4.500 h=0.16 axis=y }

# THE RADIATION. Twenty-four bands of porphyry on the twenty-four lines the coffers above are set
# out on, so the floor and the vault are the same figure at opposite ends of the room and standing
# under the oculus you can see that they are; and twenty-four of verde in the outer field, in the
# middles between them, where there is room for them.
#
# Bands of constant width, not wedges, because a band through the axis is the same width at every
# radius and the FIELD between two of them widens on its own, which is what radiating actually
# looks like. THE TWO SETS ARE IN DIFFERENT ANNULI ON PURPOSE. Forty-eight bands in one field is
# fifty-seven per cent of the pavement at its inner edge, and the first pass of this floor came
# back as a fairground awning — red, white and blue stripes with no ground left between them for
# the eye to rest on. Colour in a room like this works by being surrounded; a coloured floor that
# is more colour than ground stops being a coloured floor and becomes a carpet.
#
# Same quadrant-and-two-folds as everything else here, same negative angles, same bounding shape.
let rotunda_spoke_a = box 1.34 1.66 -0.0675   3.61 1.81 0.0675
let rotunda_sa_b = rotate { rotunda_spoke_a } y=-0.0416667
let rotunda_sa_c = rotate { rotunda_spoke_a } y=-0.0833333
let rotunda_sa_d = rotate { rotunda_spoke_a } y=-0.125
let rotunda_sa_e = rotate { rotunda_spoke_a } y=-0.1666667
let rotunda_sa_f = rotate { rotunda_spoke_a } y=-0.2083333
let rotunda_sa_g = rotate { rotunda_spoke_a } y=-0.25
let rotunda_spokes_a = intersection {
    mirror { mirror { union { rotunda_spoke_a rotunda_sa_b rotunda_sa_c rotunda_sa_d
                              rotunda_sa_e rotunda_sa_f rotunda_sa_g } } axis=x } axis=z
    rotunda_fl_field
}

let rotunda_spoke_b = box 3.72 1.66 -0.045   4.51 1.81 0.045
let rotunda_sb_a = rotate { rotunda_spoke_b } y=-0.0208333
let rotunda_sb_b = rotate { rotunda_spoke_b } y=-0.0625
let rotunda_sb_c = rotate { rotunda_spoke_b } y=-0.1041667
let rotunda_sb_d = rotate { rotunda_spoke_b } y=-0.1458333
let rotunda_sb_e = rotate { rotunda_spoke_b } y=-0.1875
let rotunda_sb_f = rotate { rotunda_spoke_b } y=-0.2291667
let rotunda_spokes_b = intersection {
    mirror { mirror { union { rotunda_sb_a rotunda_sb_b rotunda_sb_c
                              rotunda_sb_d rotunda_sb_e rotunda_sb_f } } axis=x } axis=z
    rotunda_fl_outer
}

paint slate      where=rotunda_fl_border   below=0.02
paint marble     where=rotunda_fl_outer    below=0.02
paint verde      where=rotunda_spokes_b    below=0.02
paint lapis      where=rotunda_fl_ring_out below=0.02
paint marble_hot where=rotunda_fl_field    below=0.02
paint porphyry   where=rotunda_spokes_a    below=0.02
paint porphyry   where=rotunda_fl_star     below=0.02
paint verde      where=rotunda_fl_ring_in  below=0.02
paint marble     where=rotunda_fl_disc     below=0.02

# THE THRESHOLD STONES, AND THE ONLY PLACE IN THIS ROOM WHERE PAINT IS ALLOWED TO SAY "USED".
#
# Three sectors of the border, 2.70 wide, one in each bay that goes anywhere — a different stone
# from the slate border they are set into, because a threshold always is: it is the piece that gets
# replaced, so it is laid in whatever was hardest and it never matches. `travertine` is the choice
# and NOTHING ELSE IN THIS BUILDING PAINTS IT indoors, so it cannot be confused with a neighbour's.
#
# And then the saucer worn into it, in `onyx` — the same warm stone family two shades along and at
# roughness 90 against travertine's 228. That is the whole of what wear IS to a renderer: not a
# darker colour, a SMOOTHER one, because a million feet polish a stone before they stain it. It is
# also a translucent at 160 lying in a hollow lit only by bounce, which is a question worth asking
# of the one part of this floor a foot has actually touched.
#
# BOTH ZONES ARE GROWN SOLIDS AND NEITHER IS THE HOLE. The saucer is cut out of the paving, so
# every voxel that lines it is a voxel the cutter MISSED — the trap this file's own note about the
# niches spends a paragraph on. `round { } by=0.05` grows the dish into the stone around it, and
# the intersection with the border keeps it off the wall. `below=0.035` and not 0.02 on both,
# because a `rotate` and a `mirror` put them where they are (BRIEF.md rule 5).
let rotunda_ts_one   = box 4.49 1.66 -1.35   5.16 1.83 1.35
let rotunda_ts_south = rotate { rotunda_ts_one } y=0.25
let rotunda_thr_zone = intersection {
    union { mirror { rotunda_ts_one } axis=x   rotunda_ts_south }
    rotunda_fl_border
}
let rotunda_worn_zone = intersection {
    round { rotunda_thresholds } by=0.05
    rotunda_fl_border
}
paint travertine where=rotunda_thr_zone  below=0.035
paint onyx       where=rotunda_worn_zone below=0.035

# THE HOLLOWS, AND WHY THESE TWO RULES ARE KEYED ON A GROWN COPY OF A VOID AND NOT ON THE VOID.
#
# The coffers are plaster, like the vault they are cut into — dome.clip's own lining rule only
# reaches 0.09 behind the intrados, which is the first course of a coffer and not the other two, so
# without this rule the pan of every coffer in the building comes back the colour of the dome's
# copper. The niches are lined in green marble, which is the second reason there is any colour in
# this room at all and the thing a gilt urn is seen against.
#
# BUT A SURFACE MADE BY A SUBTRACTION LIES OUTSIDE THE THING THAT SUBTRACTED IT. Every voxel of a
# niche's lining is a voxel the cutter did NOT contain — it is the first one it missed — so its
# centre stands up to half a voxel outside, which is 0.0156 at the contract's metre 32 and 0.042
# at the metre 12 anybody previews at. `below=0.02` catches the first and misses the second, and
# the niches came back grey with a green ring round the mouth, which is exactly the half of the
# lining that happened to fall inside. The brief's 0.02 is right and is not the thing to change;
# what is wrong is keying a paint rule on a HOLE. Both rules below therefore name a zone that
# CONTAINS the stone to be painted rather than the air that was taken out of it — a band a couple
# of module fractions thick, lying behind the surface, buried where nothing can see it. The
# architrave and the urns are painted after these and win back the 0.27 of wall face round each
# niche mouth that the niche zone reaches out onto.
#
# The obvious way to write that is `offset { rotunda_coffers } by=-0.045` — grow the hole. These
# zones are NOT written that way, but only because they are cheaper drawn from scratch, and NOT
# for the reason an earlier version of this comment gave. That version claimed `offset` cost
# fifteen times the sample time by throwing away its child's bounding box, with numbers. The
# numbers were wrong: five other agents were building at the same time and I had compared two runs
# minutes apart. Measured properly — the three variants written alternately in one session, four
# rounds each, medians — they are the same shape at the same price:
#
#   the zones drawn from scratch (this)      749 ms    1,728,878 field evaluations
#   offset { the void }                      784 ms    1,725,873
#   intersection { offset { the void } box } 751 ms    1,725,873
#
# So: `offset` of a bounded shape is fine, and if you are going to quote a timing on a machine
# somebody else is also using, interleave the variants or do not quote it.
#
# What the zones ARE drawn from, and why it is still the better way to say it: a coffer can never
# be deeper than 0.225, so the shell from the intrados out to 0.30 (2M/3) contains every coffer
# surface in the dome and costs two ellipsoids instead of thirteen banded shells and a rib fan;
# and the niche's own 0.945 cutter — the one the architrave is struck from — already contains the
# whole of the lining and 0.27 of the wall face round the mouth, which the architrave rule below
# paints back to marble. Both reach into stone that nothing can see.
let rotunda_int_d = ellipsoid 0 15.60 0  rx=5.100 ry=2.450 rz=5.100
let rotunda_cof_zone = intersection {
    difference { rotunda_int_d rotunda_int }
    box -5.15 15.60 -5.15   5.15 18.10 5.15
}

let rotunda_nz_r = rotate { rotunda_niche_big } y=-0.125
let rotunda_niche_zone = intersection {
    mirror { mirror { rotunda_nz_r } axis=x } axis=z
    box -5.60 2.35 -5.60   5.60 6.40 5.60
}

paint plaster where=rotunda_cof_zone   below=0.02
paint verde   where=rotunda_niche_zone below=0.02

# WHAT STANDS IN THEM. Last, so nothing above reaches them.
paint marble   where=rotunda_dressings below=0.02
paint porphyry where=rotunda_pedestals below=0.02
paint gilt     where=rotunda_urns      below=0.02

# GILT AND NOT BRONZE, and the reason is the light and not the taste. Bronze is 112,88,52 at
# roughness 110; against a verde lining at 62,92,74 in a room with no direct light in it, the urn
# came back as a brown smudge on a green ground and there was nothing in it for a renderer to be
# right or wrong about. Gilt is 204,164,84 at roughness 64 and metal 225 — a near-polished metal
# standing in a hemisphere that is lit ONLY by what bounced off a floor twelve metres away. What
# it reflects is the room, and whether it reflects the room is the question. It is also the only
# gilt below the roof: everywhere else in this building it is on a finial against the sky.

# NO WEATHERING. This is an interior, it has never been rained on, and `weather` puts its coats on
# by a VALUE rather than by a place — it would bleach the coffers and the urns along with the wall.
# Deliberate, and written down so the next person does not read the absence as an oversight.
#
# THE ONE DISPLACEMENT IN THIS FILE IS ON `rotunda_wall_t` AND ON NOTHING ELSE, which is the same
# argument arriving at a different answer rather than a change of mind. What the original note
# refused was a displacement over the whole PART, because that reaches the coffers, and their
# 0.0225 steps are half of what this room exists to carry. A displacement on the bare revolved wall
# — before a single bay, niche or panel is cut out of it and before the order is stood against it —
# cannot reach a coffer, cannot reach a moulding and cannot reach an arris. It reaches exactly the
# 300 m² of laid marble that has no business being a mathematically perfect cylinder, and 0.030 is
# 0.96 of a voxel at metre 32: past the sampler's half-voxel floor, so it moves the surface instead
# of dithering it, which is what the manifest's own dropped 0.012 could not do.
